===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A {{l|en|thing}} that has {{l|en|physical}} {{l|en|existence}} but is not alive.
# {{l|en|objective|Objective}}; {{l|en|goal}}, {{l|en|end}} or {{l|en|purpose}} of something.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1825|publisher=Great Britain. Parliament. House of Lords|title=Accounts and Papers|page=91
|passage=Money is an '''Object''' to you?<br>Money is an '''Object''' to me.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1860|author=Thomas Fenner Curtis|title=The Progress of Baptist Principles in the Last Hundred Years|page=161
|passage=And yet it may be proper to show that if time were an '''object''', little, if any thing, would necessarily be gained by sprinkling in place of immersion, where a large  number had to be baptized.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1863|author=Great Britain. Parliament. House of Commons|title=Reports from Committees|page=240
|passage={{...}} to secure first-class men you must either hold out a temptation of money, if money is an '''object''' to them, or if it is not, then after a certain number of years' service, perhaps, some honour to be bestowed upon them; one or the other, I think, ought to be given to secure the best men that you can.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1877|author=South Australia. Parliament|title=Proceedings of the Parliament of South Australia: With Copies of Documents Ordered to be Printed ...|page=29
|passage=I think, if a captain had plenty of time to spare, and was not going on to any other port, he would prefer going into harbor; but if time were an '''object''' with him, and he wished to get away as quickly as possible, he would go to the pier outside.}}
#* '''2000,''' Phyllis Barkas Goldman & John Grigni, ''Monkeyshines on Ancient Cultures''
#*: The '''object''' of tlachtli was to keep the rubber ball from touching the ground while trying to push it to the opponent's endline.
# {{senseid|en|n-grammar}} {{lb|en|grammar}} The {{l|en|noun phrase}} which is an internal {{l|en|complement}} of a {{l|en|verb phrase}} or a {{l|en|prepositional phrase}}. In a verb phrase with a {{l|en|transitive}} action verb, it is typically the receiver of the action.
#: {{hypo|en|direct object|indirect object}}
#: {{cot|en|subject<id:n-grammar>|;|agent<id:grammar>|patient<id:linguistics>|rheme<id:linguistics>|theme<id:linguistic counterpart to rheme>}}
# A person or thing toward which an emotion is directed.
#: {{ux|en|Mary Jane had been the '''object''' of Peter's affection for years.}}
#: {{ux|en|The convertible, once the '''object''' of his desire, was now the '''object''' of his hatred.}}
#: {{ux|en|Where's your '''object''' of ridicule now?}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly in the negative}} A cause for [[attention]] or [[concern]].
##: {{syn|en|focus}}
##: {{co|en|the '''object''' of a strongly worded censure}}
##: {{ux|en|Money is no '''object''' to him.}}
# {{senseid|en|compsci}} {{lb|en|object-oriented programming}} An {{l|en|instantiation}} of a {{l|en|class}} or {{l|en|structure}}.
# {{lb|en|category theory}} An {{l|en|instance}} of one of the two kinds of {{l|en|entities}} that form a {{l|en|category}}, the other kind being the {{l|en|arrow}}s (also called {{l|en|morphism}}s).
#: {{ux|en|Similarly, there is a category whose '''objects''' are groups and whose arrows are the homomorphisms from one group to another.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Sight; show; appearance; aspect.
#* '''c. 1610s''', {{w|George Chapman}}, ''Batrachomyomachia''
#*: He, advancing close / Up to the lake, past all the rest, arose / In glorious '''object'''.

