===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*weyḱ-}}
Borrowed from {{bor|en|it|villa}}, from {{der|en|la|vīlla||country house}}. {{doublet|en|vill|ville}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[house]], often [[larger]] and more [[expensive]] than [[average]], in the [[countryside]] or on the [[coast]], often used as a [[retreat]].
#* {{RQ:en:CSL|volume=1|page=3|text=What I commissioned you to get for me, and anything you see suitable to my Tusculan '''villa''', I should be glad if you will, as you say in your letter, procure for me, only don't put yourself to any inconvenience. The truth is, there is no other place that gives me complete rest after all my worries and hard work.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1922|author=w:Michael Arlen|title=“Piracy”: A Romantic Chronicle of These Days|chapter=3/6/1|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1519647W
|passage=This '''villa''' was long and low and white, and severe after its manner : for upon and about it were none of those playful ebullitions of taste, such as conical towers, domed roofs, embattlements, statues, coloured tiles and crenellations, such as are dear to architects of '''villas''' all the world over.}}
# {{lb|en|UK}} A [[family]] house, often [[semi-detached]] in [[Victorian]] or [[Edwardian]] style, in a [[middle class]] [[street]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1905|author=w:E. Nesbit|title=The Railway Children|chapter=1|url=|page=1|text= They were just ordinary suburban children, and they lived with their Father and Mother in an ordinary red-brick-fronted '''villa''', with coloured glass in the front door, a tiled passage that was called a hall, a bath-room with hot and cold water, electric bells, French windows, and a good deal of white paint, and 'every modern convenience', as the house-agents say.}}
# {{lb|en|Nigeria|slang}} One’s [[village]] or [[ancestral]] [[homeland]].

