==English==
[[File:2012-01-09 Chikungunya on the left feet at The Philippines.jpeg|right|thumb|[[Rash]] on the [[foot]] of a person with chikungunya]]

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|kde|chikungunya||that which bends up}},<ref name="MWO">{{R:MWO}}</ref> apparently referring to the [[arthrosis|arthritic effects]] of the disease. See {{m|kde|-kunja}}, a suffix related to bending.<ref>{{cite-book
|author=Pieter Jacob Kraal
|editor=Universiteit Leiden
|title=A Grammar of Makonde
|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240218145758/https://theswissbay.ch/pdf/Books/Linguistics/Mega%20linguistics%20pack/African/Niger-Congo/Bantu/Makonde%2C%20A%20Grammar%20of%20%28Kraal%29.pdf
|location=Chinnima, Tanzania
|year=2005
|page=198 (book) or 212 (PDF)
|isbn=9789090198675
|text=-kunja ... bend.}}</ref> Compare {{cog|sw|kunja||to bend, to fold}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/t͡ʃɪkʊŋˈɡʊnjə/|/ˌt͡ʃɪkənˈɡʌnjə/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-chikungunya.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# A viral [[fever]] caused by the {{taxfmt|Chikungunya virus|species}}, an [[alphavirus]] spread by [[mosquito]] bites. {{defdate|from 1952|refn=MWO}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Didier Fontenille; Anna Bella Failloux; Roberto Romi|chapter=Should we expect '''Chikungunya''' and Dengue in Southern Europe?|editors=Willem Takken; Bart G. J. Knols|title=Emerging Pests and Vector-borne Diseases in Europe|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=BEs_2o58GSkC&pg=PA169&dq=%22chikungunya%22%7C%22chikungunyas%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=tj1iU7rnJoX28QWkv4GYBQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22chikungunya%22%7C%22chikungunyas%22&f=false|page=169
|passage='''Chikungunya''' and [[dengue]] [[arbovirus|arboviral]] diseases are in expansion throughout the world.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Stephen A. Berger|title=Infectious Diseases of Zimbabwe|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=SmofSKBd1iUC&pg=PA55&dq=%22chikungunya%22%7C%22chikungunyas%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=tj1iU7rnJoX28QWkv4GYBQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22chikungunya%22%7C%22chikungunyas%22&f=false|page=55
|passage=The [[fever]] of '''Chikungunya''' is characterized by a rapid rise in temperature to as high as 40 C, often accompanied by [[rigor]]s, [[myalgia]], [[headache]], [[photophobia]], [[retro-orbital pain]], [[sore throat]] with objective signs of [[pharyngitis]], [[nausea]], and [[vomit]]ing.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|author=Franz J. Conraths; Thomas C. Mettenleiter|title=Chapter 13: Infectious Diseases Under the Influence of Changing Environmental Factors|pageurl=http://books.google.com.au/books?id=IFy_KhR2D78C&pg=PA256&dq=%22chikungunya%22%7C%22chikungunyas%22&hl=en&sa=X&ei=tj1iU7rnJoX28QWkv4GYBQ&redir_esc=y#v=onepage&q=%22chikungunya%22%7C%22chikungunyas%22&f=false|page=256
|passage='''Chikungunya''' infections are caused by an alphavirus from the family '''{{taxfmt|Togaviridae|family|i=1}}'''.}}

