# {{lb|en|historical}} A [[personal]] [[epithet]] or [[clan]] [[name]] [[add]]ed to the [[given name]] and [[family name]] of [[Ancient Romans]].
#: {{syn|en|byname|surname|to-name<qq:archaic, inexact>}}
#: {{coord|en|praenomen<qq:given name>|nomen<qq:family name>|agnomen<qq:epithet>}}
#: {{ux|en|{{w|Julius Caesar}}'s actual name was Gaius Iulius Caesar. Gaius was his [[praenomen]] or [[forename]], Iulius his [[nomen]] or [[surname]], and Caesar his '''cognomen''', denoting which part of the Iulius family he belonged to.}}
#* {{RQ:Haggard She|passage="Five hundred years or more afterwards, the family migrated to Rome under circumstances of which no trace remains, and here, probably with the idea of preserving the idea of vengeance which we find set out in the name of Tisisthenes, they appear to have pretty regularly assumed the '''cognomen''' of Vindex, or Avenger."}}
#* {{RQ:Withycombe Christian Names|edition=1st|chapter=Introduction|page=xiv|passage=The Romans evolved a quite different system of nomenclature, which in its classical form consisted of three names, the praenomen (e.g. Marcus), nomen (e.g. Tullius), and '''cognomen''' (e.g. Cicero), and two other designations (the name of the father and of the tribe): {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=[[w:David Potter (historian)|David Potter]]|title=The Emperors of Rome|chapter=1|url=|page=36|text=Roman tradition suggests that he might also have had the '''''cognomen''''' Octavian to indicate his biological family.}}
# {{lb|en|literary|_|or|_|jocular}} {{synonym of|en|nickname}}, any [[epithet]] [[used]] [[similar]] to the [[Roman]] cognomina.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1820|year_published=1864|title=[[s:en:The Legend of Sleepy Hollow (1864)|The Legend of Sleepy Hollow]]|author=w:Washington Irving|passage=In this by-place of nature, there abode, in a remote period of American history, that is to say, some thirty years since, a worthy wight of the name of Ichabod Crane; who sojourned, or, as he expressed it, "tarried," in Sleepy Hollow{{nb...}}. The '''cognomen''' of Crane was not inapplicable to his person.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Lady Anne Granard|page=237|volume=I|passage=Her husband was evidently a sensible man, and he might have given his wife a little more sense than she could have derived from her downright father and her silly mother-in-law, who were really as great a pair of noodles as ever were exhibited in the pages of a modern novel, under the '''cognomen''' of "amiable rustics."}}
# {{lb|en|literary|_|or|_|jocular|uncommon}} {{synonym of|en|surname}}, a [[family name]].
#* {{quote-av
|en
|speaker=w:Evan Davis
|title=[[w:Dragons' Den (British TV programme)|Dragons' Den]]
|date=2018-12-23
|passage=What's in a name? Well, to the Dragons, it would seem rather a lot, as they've tonight committed their cash to personalised products and to the man with the most famous '''cognomen''' in confectionery. I'll leave you to look that one up.}}

====Usage notes====
As officially used, Roman cognomina were typically not descriptive of any given person but were carried down from a famous ancestor so described, particularly those who held a high office like consul under the Republic, and indicate that ancestor's branch of the larger family. Personal epithets are sometimes further distinguished as {{m|en|agnomen|agnomina}}, in which case ''cognomen'' is only used to describe such clan names.

